        "fixed-setting": "Connected compact Hermitian X, fixed positive smooth volume, nonzero nonnegative L1 density; all limits use the same f.",
        "greatest-selection": "At every admitted exponent an exact solution is a subsolution, so the retained theorem supplies a greatest solution dominating every genuine subsolution.",
        "upper-interval-premise": "The retained upper-interval proposition implies every exponent above the finite infimum of a nonempty set is admitted.",
        "uniform-barrier-parameter": "The parameter tau is any strictly positive number, whether or not tau belongs to the admitted set.",
        "bounded-truncation": "q=min(f,1) is bounded, nonnegative and nonzero, hence in every finite Lp on compact X.",
        "Nguyen-premises": "The bounded nonzero density and positive tau satisfy the retained continuous exponential-solvability theorem.",
        "upward-translation": "t=w+max(0,-inf w) is continuous h-psh, nonnegative and has the same Monge-Ampere measure as w.",
        "supersolution-direction": "w<=t and t>=0 imply exp(tau w)q<=exp(tau t)f<=exp(lambda t)f for lambda>=tau.",
        "strict-set-factor": "On t<u-epsilon, exp(lambda t)f<=exp(-lambda epsilon)exp(lambda u)f; the subsolution inequality gives the strict domination hypothesis.",
        "domination-background": "Hermitian h is semipositive and big, as required by the retained singular strict-domination theorem.",
        "smaller-envelope": "The constant inf t is a competitor for P_(h/2)(t), so this envelope is genuine.",
        "singularity-order": "Every genuine quasi-psh u has finite supremum while t is bounded below, yielding u-epsilon<=t+C and hence u-epsilon precedes t.",
        "strict-constant": "For positive lambda and epsilon, exp(-lambda epsilon)<1; domination gives t>=u-epsilon and passage epsilon->0 gives the upper bound.",
        "upper-bound-sign": "K=sup t is finite and nonnegative; the sign is retained in every exponent and logarithmic estimate.",
        "shifted-order": "delta=K(lambda2-lambda1)/lambda2 is nonnegative and finite. Since U_lambda1<=K, lambda2(U_lambda1-delta)<=lambda1 U_lambda1.",
        "cross-exponent-subsolution": "Constant invariance and the previous scalar inequality make U_lambda1-delta a genuine subsolution at lambda2; no potential scaling is used.",
        "greatest-order": "Maximality of the greatest subsolution supplies U_lambda2>=U_lambda1-delta without a singular comparison between the two unbounded solutions.",
        "logarithmic-inequality": "For x>=1, log x>=1-1/x, since their difference vanishes at 1 and has derivative (x-1)/x^2>=0.",
        "corrected-direction": "Adding K log(lambda/tau) makes the greatest family nondecreasing as lambda increases; it therefore decreases along lambda_j downarrow a.",
        "positive-endpoint": "For nonempty Lambda with infimum a>0, a is finite and all lambda>a are admitted. Zero is not treated as a positive endpoint.",
        "attained-lower-barrier": "An exact solution u_a shifted downward to s<=0 is a genuine subsolution at every lambda>=a. The greatest family dominates this same s.",
        "endpoint-sequence": "Choose lambda_j=a+1/j and the greatest solutions; their suprema are bounded below by sup s.",
        "common-upper-for-arbitrary-solutions": "The uniform barrier applies to any exact solution, not just the greatest family.",
        "finite-suprema-subsequence": "The hypothesized lower bound and K_a give a compact real interval for sup u_j, so a subsequence has finite limit M.",
        "normalized-compactness": "Normalized u_j-sup u_j have a genuine h-psh L1 cluster v; adding the finite scalar limit gives genuine L1 limit u.",
        "almost-everywhere-extraction": "L1 convergence admits a further dV-almost-everywhere convergent subsequence; genuine potentials are finite dV-almost everywhere.",
        "density-domination": "lambda_j<=lambda1, u_j<=K_a and K_a>=0 bound all exponential densities by exp(lambda1 K_a)f.",
        "density-L1-limit": "At finite almost-everywhere points lambda_j u_j->a u; dominated convergence identifies the L1 density limit exp(a u)f.",
        "lower-density-closure": "The retained closure lemma uses common upper bound, genuine L1 potential limit and L1 density convergence to give only MA(u)>=exp(a u)f.",
        "endpoint-exact-existence": "A genuine endpoint subsolution yields an exact greatest solution by the retained theorem; no equality of MA(u) is inferred directly from L1 compactness.",
        "excluded-endpoint-escape": "Failure of sup u_j->-infinity would yield a subsequence with uniformly bounded-below suprema and contradict the endpoint criterion.",
        "positive-exponent-collapse": "Once sup u_j<0, lambda_j>=a>0 gives exp(lambda_j u_j)<=exp(a sup u_j)->0, including value zero at poles.",
        "excluded-endpoint-mass": "Exact nonnegative finite mass is at most exp(a sup u_j)||f||1, so it converges to zero without a total-mass order assumption.",
        "admitted-right-lower-bound": "For a admitted and K=K_a, corrected V_j lie above U_a by the logarithmic order estimate including lambda1=a.",
        "genuine-monotone-limit": "Decreasing h-psh V_j have genuine lower bound U_a and integrable upper bound V1, so their limit is genuine.",
        "L1-monotone-limit": "Dominated convergence between the integrable bounds gives L1 convergence, independently of the capacity lemma.",
        "capacity-monotone-limit": "The retained monotone-potential capacity lemma applies to the genuine decreasing limit. Constants K log(lambda_j/a)->0 preserve L1 and capacity convergence.",
        "nonpositive-translation": "U_lambda_j-K<=0, and the limiting V-K is nonpositive; constant invariance preserves the equation measures.",
        "positive-closedness-premises": "The exponent limit a is positive; exp(lambda_j K)f->exp(aK)f in L1; dV is finite and non-pluripolar; capacity convergence was already proved.",
